Next is **7.35 — NPC-to-NPC Social Simulation**, where we'll define how all of these systems continue operating without the player present: NPC friendships forming, conflicts happening, romances developing, favors being exchanged, rumors spreading, alliances shifting, and how we simulate enough off-screen activity to make the world feel alive without trying to run every conversation individually.

## Ruleset 7.35 — NPC-to-NPC Social Simulation

**Status: Provisional, pending your approval.**

The governing principle is:

> **NPC social life should continue off-screen through causal, relevance-weighted simulation, with detail expanded only when events materially affect important characters, relationships, groups, or future play.**

### NPCs do not pause when the player leaves

NPCs can continue to:

- work;
- socialize;
- argue;
- date;
- make friends;
- reconcile;
- gossip;
- exchange favors;
- form opinions;
- change alliances.

The social world should not freeze outside the player's current scene.

---

# Same rules, lower resolution

NPC-to-NPC relationships should use the same underlying systems we've already established.

The difference is **simulation detail**.

On-screen:

> We might simulate the conversation, emotional reactions, and resolution directly.

Off-screen:

> “Over several weeks, Karn and Rena gradually become closer friends through repeated missions.”

The underlying causality stays consistent without generating every conversation.

---

# No protagonist-centered social world

NPCs must be free to form relationships completely independently of the player.

They may:

- prefer another NPC;
- date someone else;
- gain a rival;
- become estranged;
- make new friends;
- form families;
- reconcile with enemies.

The player is important because they're a person in the world—not because everyone else's social life waits for them.

---

# Relevance-weighted simulation

We shouldn't simulate every NPC at equal depth.

### High relevance

Detailed social development for:

- major NPCs;
- close friends;
- teammates;
- rivals;
- partners;
- antagonists;
- important leaders.

### Medium relevance

Broader state changes for:

- supporting NPCs;
- recurring contacts;
- relevant group members.

### Low relevance

Background social state for:

- unrelated civilians;
- distant acquaintances;
- incidental NPCs.

This follows our existing simulation-tier philosophy.

---

# Event-driven simulation

We do **not** need:

> simulate every NPC conversation every day.

Instead, meaningful social development occurs around events such as:

- missions;
- prolonged proximity;
- arguments;
- festivals;
- injury;
- crises;
- promotions;
- rumors;
- romantic opportunities;
- betrayal;
- group reorganization.

Routine periods can be compressed.

---

# Routine social drift

Relationships can still change slowly through ordinary life.

Examples:

> Two coworkers gradually become friendly.

> Former teammates slowly lose touch.

> Two squadmates develop mild irritation from recurring habits.

These don't require discrete scenes unless they later become important.

---

# Actual opportunity is required

NPCs can only interact if they have a plausible pathway.

That might come from:

- shared workplace;
- squad;
- family;
- neighborhood;
- social network;
- communication channel;
- event;
- deliberate meeting.

Two unrelated NPCs living on opposite sides of the world shouldn't suddenly become friends without an actual connection.

---

# Existing state carries forward

Every interaction begins from existing:

- Trust;
- Affection;
- Familiarity;
- Loyalty;
- Resentment;
- Hostility;
- Romantic Interest;
- memories.

We never regenerate the relationship every time two NPCs meet.

---

# Personality and goals drive interaction

NPCs shouldn't merely collide randomly.

They may seek interaction because they want:

- information;
- help;
- friendship;
- romance;
- reconciliation;
- revenge;
- emotional support;
- status.

That produces much stronger causal social development.

---

# When relationships are most likely to change

Change becomes more likely when NPCs experience:

- repeated contact;
- strong emotional events;
- conflict;
- vulnerability;
- shared success;
- shared hardship;
- mutual attraction;
- meaningful obligation.

Simple proximity should usually produce much slower change.

---

# Off-screen friendships

NPCs can become friends through:

- shared interests;
- work;
- missions;
- introductions;
- mutual support;
- repeated positive interaction.

But deep friendship still requires meaningful history.

We shouldn't compress:

> acquaintance → best friend

just because several months passed.

---

# Off-screen rivalry

Rivalries can emerge through:

- repeated competition;
- comparison;
- ideological disagreement;
- status conflict;
- jealousy.

They remain subject to all the rivalry rules we've already built.

---

# Off-screen romance

NPCs can independently:

- become attracted;
- flirt;
- date;
- form relationships;
- reject one another;
- separate.

But there is an important anti-simulation shortcut:

> **Single NPC + Single NPC ≠ automatic romance candidate.**

We still require:

- plausible attraction;
- opportunity;
- reciprocal interest;
- willingness.

---

# Compressed romance progression

If exact scenes don't matter, an off-screen romance might be stored as:

> attraction developed → mutual flirting → several dates → relationship established

without simulating every dinner.

If that relationship later becomes important, we preserve enough milestones and memories to expand it consistently.

---

# Conflict can happen without the player witnessing it

NPCs may:

- argue;
- betray one another;
- repair relationships;
- become estranged.

Later, the player may discover:

> “They haven't spoken in three months.”

That shouldn't mean the engine invented the feud that moment.

There should be a causal off-screen history.

---

# Favors and promises continue too

NPCs may:

- make promises;
- save each other;
- owe favors;
- repay debts.

Only meaningful obligations need persistent records.

We still avoid turning everyday kindness into bookkeeping.

---

# Rumors naturally propagate off-screen

The rumor system from 7.21 can continue through:

- families;
- teams;
- markets;
- hospitals;
- institutions.

The player doesn't need to hear each intermediate conversation.

---

# Social networks can expand naturally

NPCs may gain:

- acquaintances;
- professional contacts;
- introductions;
- family connections;
- romantic-network ties.

This updates the network system from 7.22.

---

# Prioritize important NPC pairs

We should spend more simulation effort on NPC pairs with:

- strong relationships;
- active conflict;
- attraction;
- shared goals;
- frequent contact;
- direct relevance to the player.

This avoids a major computational problem.

---

# Pair explosion prevention

If a village has 100 tracked NPCs, there are nearly 5,000 possible two-person combinations.

We absolutely should **not** process all of them.

Instead process:

> **Existing active ties + plausible new ties + event-relevant pairs**

That keeps the simulation scalable.

---

# Social radius

Each NPC effectively has a social radius defined by:

- where they live;
- where they work;
- squad;
- family;
- routine;
- profession;
- existing network.

Most new relationships form inside that radius.

Bridge contacts allow expansion beyond it.

---

# New relationships need a cause

A new connection might come from:

- assignment to same mission;
- being introduced;
- shared workplace;
- living nearby;
- attending the same event;
- family connection.

Random relationship creation should still be contextual.

---

# Time compression

If several months pass, we can process broader developments such as:

> “Repeated training gradually increases Familiarity and professional Trust.”

instead of simulating 90 training sessions.

Exact scenes become necessary only when something important happened.

---

# Store only salient social events

Detailed records should be reserved for events that:

- materially changed a relationship;
- created an important memory;
- created an obligation;
- spread information;
- changed a social network;
- altered future behavior.

Ordinary conversation usually doesn't need permanent logging.

---

# Important hidden event records

For major off-screen events, we can preserve:

> **Participants**
> **Time**
> **Context**
> **Intent**
> **Outcome**
> **Relationship changes**
> **Memories created**
> **Information transferred**
> **Obligations created**
> **Publicity**
> **Future hooks**

That lets later scenes remain consistent.

---

# No retroactive convenience

This is a particularly important rule.

The engine should not suddenly decide:

> “Actually, Dagar has secretly been best friends with this NPC for ten years.”

simply because it would solve the current story.

Important hidden relationships need a plausible history.

---

# Soft reconstruction for low-detail NPCs

For background NPCs, some missing history can be filled in when they become relevant.

But it must fit:

- age;
- location;
- profession;
- previous appearances;
- social network;
- world history.

That's **simulation completion**, not unrestricted retconning.

---

# Promotion to higher detail

If a background NPC suddenly becomes important:

- Preserve everything already known.
- Expand their important relationships.
- Generate plausible missing context.
- Never contradict established behavior merely to make them interesting.

This connects directly back to 7.2.

---

# Demotion works too

If someone becomes less important, preserve:

- core identity;
- major relationships;
- important memories;
- secrets;
- obligations;
- major goals.

Routine detail can compress.

We shouldn't lose meaningful state simply because they haven't appeared recently.

---

# Off-screen causes should produce on-screen consequences

An event the player never witnessed may later affect:

- NPC mood;
- available information;
- mission cooperation;
- reputation;
- social access;
- team Cohesion.

The player experiences the **effects** of a world that continued without them.

---

# The player shouldn't automatically receive an off-screen changelog

They may discover changes through:

- gossip;
- direct conversation;
- changed behavior;
- public announcements;
- introductions.

For example, the game doesn't immediately announce:

> “Karn and Rena are dating now.”

unless the player's character actually has some reason to know.

---

# Surprise is allowed; arbitrary twists aren't

The player may legitimately be surprised to discover something that happened while they were gone.

But that surprise should come from:

- hidden information;
- unseen opportunity;
- independent NPC agency.

Not:

> “We needed a plot twist.”

---

# NPCs must initiate actions themselves

NPCs can independently:

- invite others somewhere;
- confront them;
- ask favors;
- apologize;
- flirt;
- request information;
- reconcile.

They should not behave like idle game objects awaiting the protagonist's interaction.

---

# NPCs can also fail to act

This matters equally.

Someone may want to confess attraction and never do it because of:

- fear;
- shyness;
- timing;
- uncertainty;
- competing priorities.

The simulation shouldn't optimize every character into perfect action.

---

# Chance meetings

Coincidences can happen.

But they should be more likely when NPCs:

- share locations;
- have overlapping routines;
- share contacts.

We should avoid repeatedly relying on impossible coincidences to move social plots forward.

---

# Social event seeds

Unresolved state can create **potential events**.

For example:

> unresolved attraction
> unpaid favor
> old rivalry
> simmering resentment
> rumor

These are event **seeds**.

They only activate when appropriate opportunity appears.

That gives us a very useful way to maintain long-term social tension without forcing immediate scenes.

---

# Emotional and physical states carry into off-screen interactions

A grieving NPC may withdraw socially.

An angry NPC may be more likely to confront someone.

An injured NPC may rely more heavily on caregivers.

Rulesets 7.8 and 4 supply those states.

This section consumes them.

---

# Hidden rolls

If an important off-screen event has genuine uncertainty, Ruleset 2 can resolve it with a hidden check.

The result should then be persisted.

That prevents later outcomes from being rerolled opportunistically.

---

# Relationship change remains proportional

Off-screen simulation does **not** get accelerated progression.

Routine interaction produces small change.

Major sacrifice, betrayal, or shared trauma can produce much larger change.

The same Ruleset 7.11 principles apply.

---

# NPC autonomy remains absolute

NPCs may make choices the player dislikes.

An NPC can:

- date someone else;
- reject another NPC;
- reconcile with the player's rival;
- stop being someone's friend;
- leave a group.

The world should not preserve NPC “availability” for the protagonist.

---

# No romance reservation

This deserves its own explicit anti-exploit rule:

> **The engine must never keep an NPC single because the player might want to romance them later.**

Their romantic life continues normally.

If the player returns years later, that NPC may now have:

- a partner;
- spouse;
- children;
- completely different feelings.

That's part of the life-simulation premise.

---

# Canon NPCs

Canon characters start from:

- canon personality;
- known relationships;
- appropriate timeline state.

But causal simulation can diverge.

If the world changes, canon relationships are not protected from consequences.

Someone can:

- become friends with a different person;
- never meet their canon partner;
- lose a canon friendship;
- form a new relationship.

Canon is baseline, not destiny.

---

# Background populations

We should simulate large populations through broad social states when possible.

For example:

> “The market district is increasingly suspicious of shinobi patrols.”

We don't need an individual opinion object for every merchant.

Specific individuals are expanded when they become relevant.

---

# Social climate

Areas or groups may develop temporary broader social climates such as:

- celebratory;
- grieving;
- suspicious;
- tense;
- politically divided.

That climate can influence many interactions without pretending everyone feels identically.

---

# Dormant social hooks

Some relationship state can sit dormant for years.

Examples:

- old unpaid favor;
- unresolved rivalry;
- hidden attraction;
- old betrayal;
- family resentment.

The state becomes active again when a relevant opportunity appears.

That's ideal for a long-running life simulator.

---

# Long-term arcs

Relationships can evolve across years through:

- aging;
- career;
- promotions;
- family formation;
- trauma;
- distance;
- accumulated shared experience.

Long-term change should still be made from actual accumulated causes.

---

# Don't overproduce drama

This is another major rule.

Off-screen simulation should **not** mean:

> Every time six months pass, three couples break up, somebody betrays somebody, and two friendships collapse.

Most social life is ordinary.

Major events occur when there are actual:

- pressures;
- personalities;
- opportunities;
- unresolved tensions.

---

# Positive development happens too

The simulation should generate:

- stable marriages;
- deepening friendships;
- successful reconciliation;
- mentorship;
- improved team Cohesion;
- supportive families.

We should never bias the simulation toward conflict merely because conflict is narratively louder.

---

# Stable relationships can stay stable

If two NPCs:

- Trust each other;
- communicate well;
- have compatible lives;
- experience no major disruption;

their relationship can remain healthy for decades.

The simulation doesn't need to destabilize them to remain interesting.

---

# Persistent state updates

Important off-screen events may modify:

- relationship dimensions;
- memories;
- beliefs;
- goals;
- obligations;
- reputation;
- network connections.

That keeps every relevant system synchronized.

---

## Social Simulation Audit

For an important off-screen development, the engine should be able to answer:

- **Why did these NPCs have contact?**
- **What did each NPC want?**
- **What relationship already existed?**
- **What meaningful event occurred?**
- **How was genuine uncertainty resolved?**
- **What changed afterward?**
- **What information changed hands?**
- **What future consequences now exist?**

If we cannot answer those questions, the off-screen development was probably too arbitrary.

---

## Governing Rule

> **NPC-to-NPC social simulation should keep the world socially alive through causal, opportunity-based, relevance-weighted development, preserving important consequences while compressing routine interaction and refusing to center every relationship around the player.**

